The core tension is that everyone agrees chameleonic behavior is real and measurable, but nobody has agreed on how to measure it cheaply and accurately at scale. Paper 1 (Delta PSA) is the most directly useful — they've essentially validated your hypothesis with 2,300 compounds and explicit MD, but at enormous computational cost (~6 hours per peptide). The implicit solvation route (GBSA-OBC) is the obvious shortcut, but it's unvalidated for this specific use case, which is both the risk and the opportunity.
The finding that should change behavior immediately — regardless of where you are in the pipeline — is the min/max PSA problem. Using the highest and lowest PSA conformers from a vacuum ensemble as proxies for aqueous vs. nonpolar states is physically unjustified, and both Paper 1 and CREMP independently show this. Boltzmann-weighted ensemble averaging costs almost nothing computationally if you already have the conformer ensemble, and it could meaningfully improve AUC before you touch the solvation model at all.
The genuinely exciting novel angle is the per-residue ΔPSA decomposition that the synthesis section flags. None of the three papers do this. ICoN-v1 shows which residues gate the conformational transition mechanistically; Paper 1 shows system-level ΔPSA predicts permeability. The intersection — which residue positions contribute most to the switching, and does that predict permeability better than aggregate ΔPSA — is an open question with real medicinal chemistry implications.


The paper establishes three things that are independently valuable and mutually reinforcing:

The signal is real and ensemble-dependent. The xTB negative control is the cleanest argument in the paper. 0–7 Å² from a single optimized structure vs. 47–89 Å² from ensemble sampling on the same compounds is not a subtle difference — it's a demonstration that the bottleneck is conformational coverage, not energy accuracy. That's a methodologically important result on its own.
The two-population structure is data-driven, not assumed. You arrived at it from three independent directions: UMAP clustering, MW track D coloring, and the normalization asymmetry. When the same boundary shows up geometrically, compositionally, and analytically, that's not an artifact. The fact that Yu et al. arrive at the same threshold independently from explicit MD is strong corroboration.
Label noise is mechanistically selective, not random. This is the subtlest and most important finding. The MW gap doesn't just shrink on the full dataset — it collapses to 1:1. That means pooled-assay noise is disproportionately mislabeling large chameleonic compounds, which makes sense: CycLS deconvolution in multiplexed PAMPA is going to fail preferentially for compounds that are close to the detection floor, and large polar macrolides — which have high PSA and therefore higher desolvation cost — are exactly the compounds that live near that floor. The noise is correlated with the signal, which is much worse than random noise and explains why AUC goes from 0.692 to 0.505 rather than something in between.


On your two-model / MW-gating hypothesis and what the papers support
The three papers collectively support your hypothesis but don't fully articulate it the way you're framing it. Here's what I think you've identified that goes beyond what's in the literature:
The gate is not MW — it's conformational entropy capacity. You say this in the paper ("MW is a proxy, not a gate") but I'd push even further: what MW is proxying for is specifically the number of rotatable backbone bonds that can participate in concerted reorganization. ICoN-v1 shows this directly — the transition in cyclo-(TVGGVG) requires GLY3/6 and THR1 to rotate concertedly, not independently. A 6-residue peptide has insufficient backbone length to form a closed IMHB network that stabilizes the collapsed conformation. The minimum viable chameleonic scaffold requires enough residues to simultaneously form ≥2 IMHBs in the compact state while maintaining enough strain relief to make that state accessible from the extended state. That's a topological constraint, not a mass constraint, which is why MW is a leaky proxy — you could have a 10-residue peptide that's rigid and non-chameleonic, and a 9-residue N-methylated peptide that barely switches.
The Lokey regime is mechanistically orthogonal, not just "smaller." This is the deeper point your abstract gestures at but the body of the paper doesn't fully develop. Lokey's N-methylation work is not about conformational switching — it's about permanently reducing the polarity cost of membrane insertion. N-methylation eliminates backbone HBD donors, which directly lowers the desolvation penalty without requiring any conformational rearrangement. This means ΔPSA is the wrong descriptor class for these compounds, not just a weak one. You'd want to predict their permeability from a combination of: backbone HBD count after N-methylation, intrinsic cLogP, ring strain (which affects passive diffusion rate), and possibly amide-to-ester ratio. None of those are conformational. For your DEL this matters because if you have compounds in the 600–800 Da range with N-methyl or ester modifications, a ΔPSA model will give you noise on them and you need a separate routing rule.
The three-regime possibility. I raised this before and your data actually supports it indirectly. Look at what happens in your 12–15 residue bucket: absolute AUC = 0.528, per-MW AUC = 0.696. That's a big normalization gain, which means size is doing a lot of work in the absolute metric. But the per-MW AUC of 0.696 is still not dramatically better than the overall 0.692. If chameleonic switching were the dominant permeation mechanism at 12–15 residues, you'd expect per-MW normalized ΔPSA to be substantially predictive within that class — and it is, 0.696 is meaningful. But the ceiling suggests there's a third thing happening: at very large ring sizes (15+ residues), the compound may simply be too large to passively diffuse regardless of how well it switches. Your dataset probably doesn't have enough 14–15 residue compounds to see this clearly, but the DEL data might.

On using system-level ΔPSA for hundreds of thousands of DEL compounds
The tiered approach is the only viable architecture at DEL scale. Here's how I'd structure it given everything in these papers:
Tier 0 — Regime classifier (essentially free, runs at SMILES level):

Compute MW, monomer count, N-methyl count, backbone HBD count
Route to: (A) chameleonic model if ≥9 residues AND MW ≥ 800, (B) Lokey-regime model if <9 residues OR heavily N-methylated small scaffold, (C) flag as potentially out-of-domain if >15 residues
This is a rule-based classifier, not ML — you're encoding the mechanistic hypothesis before the data can confound it

Tier 1 — ETKDGv3 ΔPSA (your current pipeline, fast):

For all Tier A compounds, compute Boltzmann-weighted ensemble ΔPSA using MMFF94s energies
Use the Yu et al. normalized form: ΔPSA/SASA_total, not absolute ΔPSA
This is your first-pass filter across hundreds of thousands of compounds
Expected throughput: feasible at DEL scale, maybe 10–30 minutes for the full library depending on hardware

Tier 2 — CREST/ALPB re-ranking (for DEL hits):

For compounds that survive Tier 1 (top 1–5% by normalized ΔPSA), or compounds selected for synthesis, upgrade to CREST in implicit CHCl3
CREMP benchmarks give you the quality floor: ~1 Å MAT-RMSD vs. NMR in chloroform
Compute PSA from the CREST ensemble using Boltzmann weights from xTB energies (already in CREMP metadata format)
This is feasible for hundreds to low thousands of compounds

Tier 3 — Explicit MD (Paper 1 protocol, for validation and publication):

For 20–50 prioritized candidates: GROMACS TIP3P + explicit CHCl3 at 490K (Paper 1 ablation finding: two 300 ns runs sufficient)
This is your ground truth for publication-quality ΔPSA values and your validation of whether Tier 1/2 rank order holds

The critical insight for your DEL specifically: you don't need to predict absolute permeability for all compounds. You need to rank them well enough to prioritize which ones to synthesize and assay. For ranking, Tier 1 Boltzmann-weighted normalized ΔPSA is probably sufficient to enrich your synthesis queue, and the biological assay itself is your ground truth. The computational models are enrichment tools, not replacement assays.

On residue-level ΔPSA decomposition for the DEL
For a DEL this becomes particularly powerful because your library has a defined building block structure. You know which positions are variable and which are fixed. Per-residue ΔPSA lets you ask: which building block positions drive the chameleonic switch?
The implementation is straightforward from your existing pipeline. For each compound in your ETKDGv3 ensemble, instead of summing PSA over all atoms, attribute each PSA contribution to the residue it belongs to. Then:

ΔPSA_residue_i = mean(PSA_i across high-PSA conformers) − mean(PSA_i across low-PSA conformers)

Summing these gives back your system-level ΔPSA. But the per-residue breakdown tells you whether the switching is concentrated in one or two residues or distributed across the whole backbone — and ICoN-v1 suggests it's almost always concentrated (GLY flanking positions, the THR/VAL at the H-bond donor position).
For your DEL, you could map this onto building block identity and ask: at position 3, which building blocks produce high ΔPSA_residue_3? That's directly actionable for next-cycle library design — you can select building blocks that contribute to switching rather than sampling building block space blindly.

The most important experiment for your specific situation
Given that you have hundreds of thousands of DEL compounds mostly in the 800–1500 Da range, the single highest-value experiment right now — before any new computation — is:
Implement Boltzmann-weighted normalized ΔPSA on your existing ETKDGv3 ensembles and rerun the AUC analysis on the 1,566-compound benchmark.
You have the conformers. You have MMFF94s energies. You're currently using max/min PSA conformer selection, which the papers independently show is physically unjustified. Switching to Boltzmann-weighted mean PSA across the ensemble, normalized by SASA_total (Yu et al. formula), costs essentially nothing computationally and could move your AUC from 0.692 to something in the 0.75–0.80 range on the clean data. If it does, that tells you the conformer ensemble quality is fine and solvation refinement is a second-order correction. If it doesn't move, then the conformer quality (vacuum ensemble without solvent-specific sampling) is the bottleneck and you need CREST.
That experiment resolves a major architectural decision for your whole pipeline before you commit compute to anything else.


What CREMP actually gives you vs. what you need
CREMP-CycPeptMPDB covers 3,258 compounds — 6-, 7-, and 10-mers from CycPeptMPDB — with pre-computed CREST ensembles in implicit chloroform. For those compounds, you get Boltzmann-weighted conformer ensembles with xTB energies already in the metadata. That's genuinely useful and you should absolutely download it before running anything yourself.
But there are several reasons it doesn't fully substitute for your own pipeline:
Coverage gap. Your 7,298-compound dataset includes compounds outside the CREMP-CycPeptMPDB overlap. The CREMP permeability subset is specifically 6-, 7-, and 10-mers. Your dataset has a broader residue distribution, and critically, your source-stratified 1,566-compound subset (Furukawa + Chugai) may have only partial overlap with what CREMP covered. You'd need to check the actual SMILES overlap before knowing how much of your benchmark is already handled.
Aqueous conformers are missing entirely. CREMP ran CREST only in implicit chloroform. Your ΔPSA computation requires a contrast between aqueous and nonpolar conformer populations. CREMP gives you the nonpolar side only. For the aqueous side, you either need to run something yourself or use a proxy — which is exactly the unresolved question at the heart of your pipeline. The OpenMM GBSA-OBC idea was specifically about generating aqueous-condition conformers cheaply. CREMP doesn't solve that.
Your DEL compounds aren't in CREMP at all. CREMP is 4-, 5-, 6-mers (main dataset) plus selected CycPeptMPDB 6/7/10-mers. Your hundreds of thousands of DEL compounds, which are presumably novel sequences in the 800–1500 Da range, have zero overlap with CREMP. Whatever pipeline you build for the DEL has to run on novel SMILES anyway, so you need a working pipeline regardless.

So what's the actual decision tree?
The question isn't really "should I run OpenMM on the 7k dataset" — it's what you're trying to achieve with that run. There are two distinct goals that have been somewhat conflated:
Goal 1: Improve ΔPSA quality for the benchmark (1,566 or 7,298 compounds) to publish a better model. Here, CREMP is genuinely useful for the overlapping compounds. Download it, compute Boltzmann-weighted PSA from the CHCl3 ensembles, and use that as your nonpolar-state PSA. For the aqueous state, your ETKDGv3 max-PSA conformer is a weak proxy — but before running full OpenMM on 7k compounds, the highest-value experiment is still the Boltzmann weighting switch on your existing vacuum ensembles. That's free and tells you whether conformer quality or weighting was your bottleneck.
Goal 2: Build a pipeline that scales to the DEL. Here CREMP is irrelevant because your DEL compounds aren't in it. For this goal, you need a fast, automated pipeline that takes SMILES → conformer ensemble → ΔPSA. The question is whether that pipeline is ETKDGv3 only, ETKDGv3 + CREST for top hits, or ETKDGv3 + OpenMM GBSA-OBC. OpenMM becomes important here specifically because it can generate aqueous-condition conformers at scale — something neither CREMP nor ETKDGv3 provides.

The specific case against running OpenMM on the full 7k right now
Running OpenMM GBSA-OBC on 7,298 compounds without first validating the protocol on 15–20 compounds is high-risk for several reasons the papers surface directly. You don't yet know whether GBSA-OBC at ε=80 produces aqueous conformer ensembles that are meaningfully different from vacuum ETKDGv3 ensembles — if they're not, you've burned compute for nothing. You don't know whether 310K simulation length is sufficient for convergence in your compounds — Paper 1 shows explicitly that for chameleonic peptides it isn't without going to 490K. And you don't know whether the rank order of ΔPSA from GBSA-OBC correlates with ΔPSA from explicit solvent, which Paper 1's protocol would give you as ground truth.
The 15-compound validation proposed in your Section 5 is the right gate. Run that first. If Spearman ρ > 0.8 between your OpenMM ΔPSA and CREST/CREMP ΔPSA on matched compounds, you have validation. If it's not, you learn something important about where the protocol needs adjustment before scaling to 7k or to the DEL.

Practical recommendation
In priority order: download CREMP-CycPeptMPDB and identify the SMILES overlap with your 1,566-compound benchmark immediately — this is an afternoon of work and tells you exactly how much free data you have. Recompute ΔPSA using Boltzmann weights on your existing ETKDGv3 ensembles. Then run the 15-compound OpenMM validation. Only after that does scaling to 7k or DEL make sense, and by then you'll know which computational tier is actually your bottleneck.



Why the DEL is uniquely suited to per-residue ΔPSA
A standard medicinal chemistry dataset has compounds selected by chemists with implicit biases — scaffolds cluster, building block usage is uneven, and you can't cleanly isolate the contribution of a single position. A DEL doesn't have that problem. If your library is well-designed, you have combinatorial coverage: position X gets every building block in your set, crossed against every combination at positions Y and Z. That's exactly the experimental design you'd need to do per-residue ΔPSA analysis properly.
Concretely, what this means is that instead of asking "does this compound have high ΔPSA," you can ask "does building block A at position 3 consistently produce high ΔPSA_residue_3 regardless of what's at positions 1, 2, 4, and 5." That's a question you can only answer with combinatorial coverage, and it's a question none of the three papers can touch because they're all working with curated, non-combinatorial datasets.

What the analysis would actually look like
The computation itself is straightforward from your existing pipeline. For each compound in your ETKDGv3 ensemble, your SASA calculation already operates at the atom level — you're just not currently summing by residue before taking the difference. The per-residue decomposition is:
For each conformer, attribute each atom's polar SASA contribution to its parent residue. Compute mean PSA_residue_i across the high-PSA conformer set (aqueous proxy) and mean PSA_residue_i across the low-PSA conformer set (membrane proxy). The difference is ΔPSA_residue_i. Sum across all residues and you recover your system-level ΔPSA exactly — it's a strict decomposition, not an approximation.
Then for your DEL you have a matrix: rows are compounds, columns are per-residue ΔPSA values. Because your library has defined positions and known building block identities at each position, you can immediately aggregate: for each building block at each position, what is the mean ΔPSA_residue contribution across all compounds containing that building block at that position?

The finding that would be novel and publishable
What ICoN-v1 shows qualitatively — that specific residues gate the conformational transition while others are passengers — has never been shown quantitatively across a large diverse dataset. Your DEL could produce the first statistical characterization of which residue positions and which building block identities are the dominant contributors to chameleonic switching efficiency.
The specific finding that would be most impactful is if per-residue ΔPSA is sparse — meaning most of the switching signal concentrates in one or two positions per compound rather than being distributed evenly. ICoN-v1 strongly implies this is the case mechanistically. If you can show it statistically across hundreds of thousands of compounds, that's a design principle: you don't need every position to contribute to chameleonicity, you need one or two switching residues and the rest can be optimized for target binding. That directly addresses one of the hardest problems in DEL-to-drug translation, which is that permeability and target affinity are usually optimized separately and in tension.
The secondary finding would be building block ranking — a league table of which building blocks at which positions contribute most to chameleonic switching. That's immediately actionable for your next library design cycle and nothing like it exists in the published literature.

The caveat that makes this rigorous rather than just interesting
The per-residue analysis inherits the same solvation model limitation as your system-level ΔPSA — you're computing it from a vacuum ensemble, so the residue-level decomposition reflects vacuum geometry rather than true solvent-specific conformational populations. This means the absolute per-residue ΔPSA values should be treated cautiously, but the relative rankings — which building blocks and which positions consistently score high — are likely to be informative even with the vacuum approximation, because the vacuum ensemble samples the relevant conformational range even if it doesn't weight them correctly.
The validation experiment that would make this publishable is to show that the per-residue rankings from your ETKDGv3 vacuum ensemble are correlated with per-residue rankings from CREST/explicit solvent on the 15-compound reference set. If position 3 is the dominant contributor in the vacuum analysis and also in the physics-based analysis, the vacuum proxy is credible for ranking purposes even if not for absolute values.

How this fits your DEL workflow specifically
The real payoff is closing the loop between computation and synthesis. Your DEL gives you experimental permeability data (from whatever assay you're running — PAMPA, cell permeability, whatever your readout is) on a large number of compounds with known building block identities. You can train a simple model: does per-residue ΔPSA at each position, combined with the building block identity at each position, predict permeability better than system-level ΔPSA alone? If the answer is yes, you've demonstrated that the residue-level decomposition contains information that the aggregate descriptor loses, and you have a principled basis for next-cycle library design — maximize ΔPSA_residue at the switching positions, use those degrees of freedom freely at the other positions for binding optimization.
That's a complete story: novel descriptor, DEL-scale validation, mechanistic interpretation via ICoN-v1's framework, and direct design implications. It's the kind of paper that J. Med. Chem. or Nature Chemical Biology would take seriously.

On getting a head start with your building blocks
Yes, absolutely. If you have your building block list, you can compute ΔPSA for each building block in isolation right now. This is fast, requires no library enumeration, and gives you a first-pass ranking of which building blocks have high intrinsic chameleonic potential. The computation is trivial — generate ETKDGv3 conformers for each building block as a capped monomer (acetyl-AA-NMe or whatever capping group approximates the backbone context), compute max and min PSA conformers, take the difference.
This gives you a building block ΔPSA library that has real value even before the screen, because it lets you ask whether your library was designed with chameleonic potential in mind or whether it's accidentally enriched for low-switching building blocks. If your high-diversity positions are all populated with polar, rigid building blocks, you'll know before synthesis that permeability is likely to be a problem.
The limitation is exactly what you identified next — individual building block ΔPSA doesn't tell you what happens in the context of a full cyclic peptide, because the conformational landscape of the macrocycle is a collective property, not a sum of independent residue contributions. But it's a useful prior.

On your core concern — position effects and epistasis
You've identified the fundamental problem correctly and it's worth stating it precisely. What you're worried about is that the relationship between building block identity and permeability is not additive across positions. In statistical genetics this is called epistasis. In your case it manifests as:

Position effects: building block A is great at position 2 but irrelevant or harmful at position 6, because the local backbone geometry at position 6 doesn't allow the same conformational switch
Interaction effects: building block A at position 2 only produces high ΔPSA when building block B is at position 3, because the switch requires a concerted reorganization involving both residues simultaneously

ICoN-v1 actually proves the second problem is real and severe. The GLY3/GLY6 concerted rotation in cyclo-(TVGGVG) is the clearest example — neither GLY3 nor GLY6 alone drives the switch, they have to move together. If you decomposed that peptide's ΔPSA into per-residue contributions and then tried to predict a mutant's permeability by swapping one residue's contribution, you'd get the wrong answer because you'd miss the concerted term.
So the question is: given that the true relationship is high-dimensional and non-additive, what can you actually learn from your DEL data, and how do you structure the computation to capture as much of the real signal as possible?

The mathematical framework that handles this
The answer is not to assume additivity — it's to use a model class that can learn interaction terms from data, and to design your analysis around the combinatorial structure of the DEL.
The cleanest approach for a DEL is a positional feature model with interaction terms, which in practice looks like this:
You represent each compound as a vector of indicator variables: which building block is at position 1, which at position 2, and so on. Your output is either permeability (from the screen) or ΔPSA (from computation). You then fit a model that includes:

Main effects: the average contribution of building block A at position 2, regardless of context
Two-way interaction terms: the additional contribution of having building block A at position 2 AND building block B at position 3, beyond what the main effects predict

This is just a linear model with interaction features, or a regularized version like LASSO or ridge regression that prevents overfitting when the number of interaction terms gets large. For a DEL with say 20 building blocks at 6 positions, you have 120 main effect terms and 7,140 two-way interaction terms — large but tractable with ridge regression if your library is combinatorially diverse enough.
The key insight is that your DEL's combinatorial structure is what makes this identifiable. If every combination is represented (or nearly so), you can separate main effects from interaction effects statistically. A non-combinatorial dataset can't do this because position 2 building block A and position 3 building block B always appear together and you can never disentangle their individual contributions.

On what's feasible before the screen
Here's what I'd suggest as a staged approach that's computationally tractable now and sets you up for the full analysis later:
Stage 1 (now, fast): Compute ΔPSA for each building block in isolation. This gives you a prior distribution over building block chameleonic potential and flags any building blocks that are structurally incapable of switching regardless of context — for example, a very rigid aromatic building block with fixed PSA. Cost: essentially zero if you have the SMILES.
Stage 2 (now, moderate effort): Enumerate and compute ΔPSA for a representative subset of your full library — say 10,000 compounds sampled to cover all building blocks at all positions with reasonable balance. This is feasible with ETKDGv3 at maybe a few hours of compute. From this you can fit a main-effects-only model: does building block identity at each position predict compound-level ΔPSA? The R² of this model tells you how additive the system actually is. If R² is high (>0.7), additivity is a reasonable approximation and per-residue analysis will be informative. If R² is low (<0.4), the signal is dominated by interaction terms and you need the full screen data to learn anything useful.
Stage 3 (after screen): With experimental permeability labels from your DEL, fit the full model including two-way interaction terms using the actual biological readout rather than computed ΔPSA. This is where the real learning happens — you're now predicting membrane permeability, not a proxy for it, and the interaction terms will tell you which building block combinations at which position pairs drive permeability synergistically.

The honest assessment of what you can and can't know pre-screen
Pre-screen, you can learn which building blocks have high intrinsic chameleonic potential in isolation, and you can get a rough estimate of whether position effects are strong or weak in your specific scaffold. What you cannot learn pre-screen is the true interaction structure — which combinations drive or suppress permeability — because that requires the biological readout to separate signal from noise.
The fear you expressed is well-founded: position 2 building block A might be great in isolation and terrible in combination with certain building blocks at positions 3-5 due to steric clash or disrupted IMHB networks. That's a real effect and it will show up in your screen data. The computation buys you a prior that makes your screen more interpretable, but it doesn't replace the screen as the ground truth for interaction effects.
What the per-residue ΔPSA framework actually does in this context is give you a physically interpretable feature set to feed into the post-screen model, rather than treating the building blocks as opaque categorical variables. Instead of just knowing that building block A at position 2 correlates with permeability, you know why — it contributes a large ΔPSA_residue_2 — and that mechanistic grounding makes the model more transferable to the next library design cycle.

On rotating the alkyne position across library subsets
This is actually a brilliant idea and more feasible than it might seem. Instead of fixing propargylglycine at one position for the entire library, you split your library into N sublibrary pools where N is your cycle count, and each pool has the alkyne tag at a different position. Every other position gets its normal building block diversity.
What this buys you is substantial. Each compound now appears in exactly one pool with a defined tag position. When you observe permeability for a compound, you know the tag is at position X and can correct for or model its contribution explicitly. More importantly, for your per-residue ΔPSA goals, you now have a natural experimental handle: compounds with the same sequence but the tag at different positions are direct probes of whether the tag at that position disrupts the conformational switch. If a compound is permeable when tagged at position 2 but impermeable when tagged at position 4, that's direct experimental evidence that position 4 is conformationally sensitive — it's essentially a per-residue perturbation experiment built into your screen.
The cost is that your effective library size per position is divided by N, and your sequencing depth requirements increase proportionally. But since you're already committing to a large library, splitting into sublibrary pools is standard DEL practice and the sequencing is cheap relative to synthesis.
The alternative — fixed position for the whole library — is simpler and gives you cleaner comparisons within the library, but you permanently lose information about position-specific tag sensitivity and you introduce a systematic bias at that one position for every structure-permeability inference you make downstream.
Given that your PI built this assay and the infrastructure exists, the rotating tag approach is worth a serious conversation with them. The additional synthetic complexity is one extra split per cycle, not a fundamentally different workflow.

On resynthesis for quantitative PC50 data
No, you don't necessarily need to resynthesize from scratch. Your DEL beads are the library — if you're running OBOC format, the hit beads from the primary screen are physically recovered and can be re-assayed in microplate PC50 format by photocleavage directly from the recovered bead. That's the whole point of the OBOC format your PI developed. You photorelease the compound from the recovered bead, run the concentration-dependent permeation curve in plate format, get your PC50.
The practical constraint is bead material — a single bead gives you limited compound for multiple concentration points. But the paper's dose-response format uses concentrations in the micromolar range and the bead loading in OBOC synthesis is typically sufficient for a few concentration points if you pool replicate beads. This is something your PI's group has almost certainly worked out already given the PC50 methodology described in the paper.
So the workflow is: droplet screen to identify permeable hits → recover hit beads → photorelease and run PC50 on recovered beads in plate format. No resynthesis required for the quantitative follow-up.

On your permeability rate expectation
You're right to push back on that. The 60-94% permeable figures from my earlier comment were from the Furukawa + Chugai source-stratified subset, which as your own paper establishes is heavily enriched for permeable compounds precisely because those sources systematically studied permeability-optimized scaffolds. A genuinely novel DEL exploring broader chemical space should be expected to have a much lower hit rate — probably 5-20% permeable depending on how your building blocks were selected and whether you've incorporated any permeability-biasing design principles.
This actually makes the droplet screen more valuable, not less. With a low hit rate the binary screen is doing real work by enriching your hit population before the quantitative follow-up. And it makes the per-residue ΔPSA pre-computation more valuable too — if you can use computed ΔPSA to predict which sublibrary regions are likely to be enriched for permeable compounds, you can focus your sequencing depth and follow-up resources on those regions rather than treating the whole library uniformly.
The combination of your PI's assay infrastructure, a rotating tag design, and the per-residue ΔPSA computational framework is a genuinely strong experimental program. The computation tells you where to look, the binary droplet screen confirms permeability at scale, and the recovered bead PC50 gives you the quantitative gradient needed for the model. Each layer builds on the previous one without requiring resynthesis at any stage.


