From this enumerated subset you compute three things per compound. First, system-level ΔPSA using Boltzmann-weighted ensemble averaging rather than min/max selection, normalized by SASA_total following the Yu et al. formula. Second, per-residue ΔPSA decomposition attributing each atom's PSA contribution to its parent building block position, giving you ΔPSA_pos1 through ΔPSA_pos5 for every enumerated compound. Third, the standard 2D descriptors — MW, HBD count, TPSA, rotatable bonds — as baseline comparators.
With this computed dataset you fit the main effects model entirely from computation, before touching the screen. Your outcome variable is computed system-level ΔPSA, your predictors are building block identities at each position. The R² of this model tells you how additive your system is computationally — if it's high, positions contribute relatively independently and your pre-screen predictions will be reliable. If it's low, interaction terms dominate and the screen data becomes more critical for learning the true structure-permeability landscape.
Critically, you also fit the linker-stratified version of this model separately — the same main effects analysis run independently within each of your 14 linker classes. Comparing the building block rankings across linker contexts tells you computationally which amino acid contributions are linker-independent robust effects versus which are highly context-dependent. This is your first window into the linker × building block interaction without any experimental data.
The per-residue ΔPSA decomposition from the enumerated subset gives you something else valuable — a prediction of which position in your scaffold is the dominant switching driver computationally. Given your library symmetry with positions 1/3 sharing building blocks and positions 2/4 sharing building blocks, you can ask whether the same building block at position 2 versus position 4 produces systematically different ΔPSA_residue values. If position 2 building blocks consistently contribute more to switching than the same building blocks at position 4, that's a scaffold geometry effect detectable purely from computation before the screen runs.
The output of Phase 1 is a ranked list of your full library's predicted chameleonic potential, stratified by linker, with per-residue decomposition identifying which positions are computationally predicted to drive switching. This becomes your prior for interpreting the screen and for designing your resynthesis set.

Phase 2: Screen and integration
During the screen itself the computational predictions serve two purposes. First, as a quality control check — if your binary permeable/impermeable calls from the droplet screen are completely uncorrelated with your computed ΔPSA predictions, something is wrong with either the computation or the assay, and you want to know that before investing in resynthesis. A Spearman correlation between computed ΔPSA rank and screen hit rate by building block class, even from a binary readout, should be detectable if the computational signal is real.
Second, your computed ΔPSA ranks inform which compounds to prioritize for resynthesis. You want three categories in your resynthesis set. The high-ΔPSA predicted, screen-confirmed permeable compounds validate that computation predicts the right things. The high-ΔPSA predicted, screen-impermeable compounds are your most interesting failures — they suggest either the computation is wrong about those building block combinations, or the linker geometry is suppressing a switch that the computation predicted. The near-threshold compounds with moderate computed ΔPSA that were borderline in the screen are where the quantitative PC50 data adds the most information, because these are exactly the compounds where the continuous permeability gradient matters for fitting the regression model.
Your target resynthesis set of roughly 140-210 compounds, stratified across your 14 linkers and designed to cover your building block main effects, gives you quantitative PC50 values as continuous labels. With these in hand you fit the full regression model — building block main effects plus the position identity × building block identity interaction terms — using PC50 as the outcome rather than binary permeability. The position × building block interaction is your 36-term question of whether building block X behaves differently at position 2 versus position 4, and 11-term question for positions 1 versus 3. This is tractable with your resynthesis set size.
The linker as a moderator variable gets incorporated by fitting the model separately within linker classes and then comparing coefficients across linkers. Building blocks whose main effect coefficients are stable across all 14 linkers are position-robust chameleonic contributors — those are your most valuable design principles for the next library because they work regardless of scaffold context. Building blocks whose coefficients vary dramatically across linkers are conformationally sensitive to ring geometry — interesting mechanistically but less reliable as design rules.

What you extract and what it means
The computational output before the screen gives you predicted chameleonic potential for every compound in your library, per-residue decomposition identifying which positions drive switching, and linker-stratified building block rankings. The screen gives you binary permeability calls at library scale validating and refining those predictions. The resynthesis gives you quantitative PC50 values for model training. The integrated model gives you building block main effects, position-specific contributions, and linker modulation coefficients that together constitute a quantitative structure-permeability map of your chemical space.
The deliverable for the next library design cycle is concrete and actionable: for each linker scaffold, a ranked list of building block combinations at positions 1-4 predicted to maximize chameleonic switching efficiency, with confidence intervals derived from how consistently those predictions held up against experimental PC50 values. That's the translation from descriptive science into predictive design, and it's what makes this project more than a characterization study.

