For X1X2X3X4X5 (macrocycles), you switch to ETKDGv3 with the macrocycle torsion flag enabled. This is where the computational cost increases but with 1.9 million SMILES you want to parallelize across your 14 linker classes — each linker class is an independent batch so you can run them simultaneously if you have access to a cluster or even just a multi-core workstation.
The per-residue attribution at each stage is the key novel computation. In RDKit you can get atom-level SASA contributions from rdFreeSASA, and every atom in your SMILES has a defined residue membership from your enumeration scheme. So for each conformer you sum the polar SASA contributions by residue, Boltzmann weight across conformers, and output ΔPSA_pos1 through ΔPSA_posN for however many positions exist at that enumeration stage.
What this gives you as output at each stage is a feature matrix where rows are compounds and columns are:

System-level ΔPSA (Boltzmann weighted, SASA normalized)
Per-residue ΔPSA for each position present at that stage
Building block identity at each position (categorical)
Standard 2D descriptors as controls
MW, HBD, TPSA for the two-regime classifier

The sequential stage outputs then let you compute the ΔPSA increment for each building block addition:
ΔPSA_increment_pos2 = ΔPSA(X1X2) - ΔPSA(X1)
ΔPSA_increment_pos3 = ΔPSA(X1X2X3) - ΔPSA(X1X2)
ΔPSA_increment_pos4 = ΔPSA(X1X2X3X4) - ΔPSA(X1X2X3)
ΔPSA_increment_linker = ΔPSA(X1X2X3X4X5) - ΔPSA(X1X2X3X4)
Each increment is directly interpretable as the marginal chameleonic contribution of adding that building block at that position in that sequence context. Averaged across all compounds containing building block X at position 2, this gives you the mean marginal ΔPSA contribution of that building block at that position — which is your empirical main effect estimate from computation rather than from experimental data.
The linker increment is particularly powerful because it's computed against the same linear precursor for all 14 linkers. So for any given X1X2X3X4 combination you get 14 ΔPSA increment values, one per linker, directly comparable because they share the identical amino acid sequence. That's a clean computational experiment isolating linker geometry effects that requires no experimental data at all.
